<p>U.S. Attorney Jeanine Pirro said investigators determined that a Secret Service officer wounded during the April 25 White House Correspondents’ Association dinner attack was struck by buckshot from a weapon belonging to Cole Tomas Allen, the man charged in the case. Pirro’s statement addressed questions about the source of the shot after earlier reports noted uncertainty in the official record. Authorities say Allen tried to storm the media gala at a Washington hotel while President Trump, journalists, administration officials and others were inside. The officer survived, Trump was evacuated safely, and Allen remains jailed pending trial on charges including attempted assassination of the president and firearms counts.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/bIfM219IjOy6m9ydy6GvJDCMpqL4G58sPWqmrZhsQmtCSVw9mWgiv28PxZMitPc0itQ1GOWdYwy_j7ONJu_FIfXj8mGAwvSZS-2wzRdKjiKNUbTrAkUIoRBMrhwDDKkvA6yIBHGUKYoyCMtHx3DWzCutWACYFCMuUtStgoYZoOM' alt='Acting Attorney General Todd Blanche, U.S. Attorney Jeanine Pirro and FBI Director Kash Patel speak during an April 27, 2026, Justice Department news conference in Washington.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Friendly-fire context: Questions arose because at least one Secret Service agent also opened fire; Pirro previously said investigators had no evidence of friendly fire before giving a more definitive account Sunday.</li><li>Sentencing exposure: Allen faces up to life in prison if convicted on the attempted assassination count alone.</li></ul><blockquote>It is definitively his bullet. - Jeanine Pirro</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>BBC News: The BBC highlighted a gap between official statements and court filings, reporting that the papers did not explicitly accuse Allen of shooting the officer. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cm2pmk0r3pjo'>BBC</a>)</li><li>The New York Times: The Times said its review of FBI video appeared to show the assailant fired first and the Secret Service officer then returned fire.